 @1ycan-eu9ji  I think it's less about absolute price and more about this level of enthusiast consumer wanting the best value; they're willing to spend, as evidenced by the 4090's sales, but not if it's overpriced. Nvidia learned all of the wrong lessons from the 3080/3090 launch and bungled the 4080/4090 pricing and specs, which is still affecting all GPU pricing.

The 3090 only had a minor performance gain over the 3080 in games, but cost 50% more. So Nvidia assumed they mispriced the 3080 and should've made it closer to the 3090 and people still would've bought it. Questionable assumption, especially when you factor in covid supply issues. So they jack up the 4080's price, bringing it closer to the 4090, except the 4090 had a sizeable performance advantage over the 4080, more than the 4080 had over the 3080 even. That made the 4080 comically overpriced and that's why it didn't sell. 

I don't know why they decided to make the 4090 so much better than the 4080, while raising the price of the 4080. Either widen the gap in performance or shrink the gap in price, not both! If the 4080 had 85% of the performance of the 4090 (and significantly, a bigger gap to the 3080/90), it would've sold at 1200.
